#1 Semantic directives

Directives express the purpose of content rather than demanding a particular visual layout. Block directives start with `&name` and end with `&end`. Official aliases such as `&n`, `&w` and `&e` are supported. This is not the same grammar as @Doc's `@note[...]`.

#2 Note — context

Notes are suitable for supplementary information and context, presented with a restrained accent.

```sm
&note[title="Further reading"]
This is supplementary information.
&end
```

&note[title="Further reading"]
This is supplementary information.
&end

#2 Tip — suggestions

Tips use a separate, low-saturation accent so readers can identify them quickly.

```sm
&tip
Check diagnostics before exporting HTML.
&end
```

&tip
Check diagnostics before exporting HTML.
&end

#2 Warning — caution

Warnings communicate consequences or risks, without filling the page with saturated colors.

```sm
&warning
Check whether a conversion will discard semantics.
&end
```

&warning
Check whether a conversion will discard semantics.
&end

#2 Aliases and unknown directives

Official aliases are explicit registry entries. Arbitrary abbreviations are not automatically inferred.

```sm
&n[title="Alias"]
Equivalent to &note.
&e
```
